    "doc_content": "# rfc4271_s5_1_1_req_03\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| RFC Reference | RFC4271-S5.1.1-REQ-03 \u2014 RFC 4271 (A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4)), \u00a75.1.1 (ORIGIN) |\n| Test Type | positive |\n| Category | path_attribute |\n| Risk | medium |\n| Priority | medium |\n| Generation Mode | ai-medium \u2014 \u26a0 **NEEDS ENGINEER REVIEW** |\n\n## Normative Statement\n\n> **SHOULD:** If the act of prepending will cause an overflow in the AS_PATH segment (i.e., more than 255 ASes), it SHOULD prepend a new segment of type AS_SEQUENCE and prepend its own AS number to this new segment. 2) if the first path segment of the AS_PATH is of type AS_SET, the local system prepends a new path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE to the AS_PATH, including its own AS number in that segment. 3) if the AS_PATH is empty, the local system creates a path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE, places its own AS into that segment, and places that segment into the AS_PATH. When a BGP speaker originates a route then: a) the originating speaker includes its own AS number in a path segment, of type AS_SEQUENCE, in the AS_PATH attribute of all UPDATE messages sent to an external peer.\n\n## Protocol Reasoning\n\nWhen a BGP speaker originates a route to an external (eBGP) peer, RFC 4271 5.1.1 requires it to include its own AS number in a path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE in the AS_PATH attribute. Since a locally-originated route starts with an empty AS_PATH, this collapses to case (3)/(a): the originator must create a single AS_SEQUENCE segment containing exactly its own ASN. On the wire this is externally observable as the AS_PATH the eBGP peer receives, where an AS_SEQUENCE renders in Junos as a bare ASN list and an AS_SET would render inside curly braces.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- **Topology:** two-router-ebgp (R1 AS 65001 \u2194 R2 AS 65002) \u2014 Default R1 (AS 65001, Junos under test as originator) <-> R2 (AS 65002, eBGP). R1 originates a route (static + export policy). The AS_PATH produced by origination is only visible in the Adj-RIB-In of the receiving peer, so the route_table observation must be taken on R2 (the eBGP peer that received the originated route), not on R1's own inet.0 where a locally-originated route carries no prepended AS_PATH.\n- **Timers:** Hold = 90, KeepAlive = 30, ConnectRetry = 120\n- **Devices:** Juniper vJunos-router / vMX pair reachable via NETCONF (PyEZ)\n\n\n## Test Steps\n\n1. Establish the eBGP session between R1 (AS 65001) and R2 (AS 65002) and confirm it reaches Established.\n2. On R1 (Junos under test), create a route to originate (e.g., a static/discard route or a directly connected prefix) and an export policy that advertises this prefix into BGP toward R2.\n3. On R2, inspect the received route's AS_PATH via the route table and verify it consists of a single AS_SEQUENCE segment containing R1's ASN (65001).\n\n## Expected Result\n\n1. The eBGP session between R1 and R2 is Established, so the originated route can actually be advertised and observed.\n2. The AS_PATH of the originated route received by the eBGP peer includes the originating speaker's own AS number (65001).\n3. The originating speaker's AS number is carried in an AS_SEQUENCE segment, not an AS_SET (Junos renders AS_SET members inside curly braces, so a conformant AS_SEQUENCE origination shows no braces).\n\n**Observation point:** `rpc.get_bgp_neighbor_information() -> .//peer-state for session; rpc.get_route_information(table='inet.0') -> .//rt-entry/as-path on the receiving peer R2 for the originated route's AS_PATH`\n\n**Additional observations captured:**\n- `received_as_path` = `.//rt-entry/as-path` via `route_table` (rpc.get_route_information(table='inet.0'))\n\n## Safety\n\n- Lab-only execution: **Yes**\n- Requires `commit confirmed` on configuration changes: **Yes**\n\n## Traceability\n\n- Knowledge base: `kb/knowledge.db` (retrieved, not re-derived from raw RFC text)\n\n\n## Notes\n\nRequirement bundles four sub-cases (overflow >255 ASes, AS_SET-first prepend, empty AS_PATH, and origination-into-AS_SEQUENCE). Only the origination case (a)/(3) is cleanly reproducible on the default two-router topology without a scriptable peer: the overflow case would need a peer emulator (ExaBGP/Scapy) to inject a route already carrying ~255 ASes, and the AS_SET-first case requires an inbound route whose AS_PATH begins with an AS_SET (typically the product of aggregation upstream), so both are out of scope here and would warrant separate tests. Confidence is medium chiefly because of the observation point: the prepend is only visible on the receiving peer's Adj-RIB-In, so the route_table call must be executed against R2, not the originating Junos device; if the harness only queries the originator, check 2/3 cannot be satisfied and the origin-code suffix (I/E/?) in the as-path text does not affect the substring checks. The '{' brace heuristic depends on Junos' as-path text rendering of AS_SET.\n\n",
