\ndef r1():\n    dev = Device(host=\"R1_LAB_HOST\", user=\"LAB_USER\", password=\"LAB_PASSWORD\", port=22)\n    dev.open()\n    yield dev\n    dev.close()\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef r2():\n    dev = Device(host=\"R2_LAB_HOST\", user=\"LAB_USER\", password=\"LAB_PASSWORD\", port=22)\n    dev.open()\n    yield dev\n    dev.close()\n\n\ndef test_rfc4271_s5_1_1_req_03(r1, r2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Traces to: RFC4271-S5.1.1-REQ-03\n    If the act of prepending will cause an overflow in the AS_PATH segment (i.e., more than 255 ASes), it SHOULD prepend a new segment of type AS_SEQUENCE and prepend its own AS number to this new segment. 2) if the first path segment of the AS_PATH is of type AS_SET, the local system prepends a new path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE to the AS_PATH, including its own AS number in that segment. 3) if the AS_PATH is empty, the local system creates a path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE, places its own AS into that segment, and places that segment into the AS_PATH. When a BGP speaker originates a route then: a) the originating speaker includes its own AS number in a path segment, of type AS_SEQUENCE, in the AS_PATH attribute of all UPDATE messages sent to an external peer.\n\n    Reasoning: When a BGP speaker originates a route to an external (eBGP) peer, RFC 4271 5.1.1 requires it to include its own AS number in a path segment of type AS_SEQUENCE in the AS_PATH attribute. Since a locally-originated route starts with an empty AS_PATH, this collapses to case (3)/(a): the originator must create a single AS_SEQUENCE segment containing exactly its own ASN. On the wire this is externally observable as the AS_PATH the eBGP peer receives, where an AS_SEQUENCE renders in Junos as a bare ASN list and an AS_SET would render inside curly braces.\n    \"\"\"\n    with Config(r1, mode=\"exclusive\") as cu:\n        cu.load(\n            '''\nprotocols {\n    bgp {\n        group EBGP-PEER {\n            type external;\n            peer-as 65002;\n            neighbor R2_LAB_IP {\n                hold-time 90;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n            ''',\n            format=\"text\",\n        )\n        cu.commit(confirm=2)\n\n    # --- stimulus ---\n    # (positive-path stimulus -- standard peering brings this up naturally)\n\n    # --- observation ---\n    # PyEZ observation point (AI-suggested): rpc.get_bgp_neighbor_information() -> .//peer-state for session; rpc.get_route_information(table='inet.0') -> .//rt-entry/as-path on the receiving peer R2 for the originated route's AS_PATH\n    _info = r1.rpc.get_bgp_neighbor_information()\n    peer_state = _info.findtext(\".//peer-state\")\n\n    # additional AI-requested observation(s)\n    _route_table = r1.rpc.get_route_information(table='inet.0')\n    received_as_path = _route_table.findtext('.//rt-entry/as-path')\n\n    # --- assertion ---\n    # Expected checks:\n    #   1. The eBGP session between R1 and R2 is Established, so the originated route can actually be advertised and observed.\n    #   2. The AS_PATH of the originated route received by the eBGP peer includes the originating speaker's own AS number (65001).\n    #   3. The originating speaker's AS number is carried in an AS_SEQUENCE segment, not an AS_SET (Junos renders AS_SET members inside curly braces, so a conformant AS_SEQUENCE origination shows no braces).\n    assert peer_state is not None, \"Could not read peer_state via PyEZ RPC\"\n    assert peer_state == 'Established', \"The eBGP session between R1 and R2 is Established, so the originated route can actually be advertised and observed. (medium confidence) -- verify against lab output\"\n    assert received_as_path is not None and '65001' in received_as_path, \"The AS_PATH of the originated route received by the eBGP peer includes the originating speaker's own AS number (65001). (medium confidence) -- verify against lab output\"\n    assert received_as_path is not None and '{' not in received_as_path and '}' not in received_as_path, \"The originating speaker's AS number is carried in an AS_SEQUENCE segment, not an AS_SET (Junos renders AS_SET members inside curly braces, so a conformant AS_SEQUENCE origination shows no braces). (medium confidence) -- verify against lab output\"\n",
