{"ai_backend":"claude-code-cli","batch_id":1,"category":"neighbor_adjacency","created_at":"2026-08-15T17:57:20","derived_from":"","doc_content":"# rfc2328_s3_req_01\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| RFC Reference | RFC2328-S3-REQ-01 \u2014 RFC 2328 (OSPF Version 2), \u00a73 (Interface Hello Protocol) |\n| Test Type | positive |\n| Category | neighbor_adjacency |\n| Risk | medium |\n| Priority | medium |\n| Generation Mode | ai-medium \u2014 \u26a0 **NEEDS ENGINEER REVIEW** |\n\n## Normative Statement\n\n> **MUST:** A router MUST send Hello packets out each OSPF-enabled interface at HelloInterval seconds.\n\n## Protocol Reasoning\n\nOSPF's Hello protocol requires each router to transmit a Hello packet out every enabled interface once per HelloInterval; these periodic Hellos are what establish and sustain neighbor relationships and reset the peer's RouterDeadInterval timer. If a Junos DUT (R1) is emitting Hellos at the correct cadence, its peer (R2) will progress and hold the adjacency at Full; a sustained Full state is indirect proof that Hellos are flowing continuously, but the exact inter-packet interval is only measurable on the wire.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- **Topology:** two-router-single-area (R1 \u2194 R2, both in Area 0.0.0.0) \u2014 Default R1 (DUT) <-> R2, both Area 0.0.0.0. A passive Scapy sniffer on the shared link is needed to timestamp successive OSPF Hello packets from R1 and confirm the ~HelloInterval spacing, since PyEZ exposes no per-packet timing.\n- **Timers:** Hello Interval = 10, Dead Interval = 40, Retransmit Interval = 5\n- **Devices:** Juniper vJunos-router / vMX pair reachable via NETCONF (PyEZ)\n- **Requires peer emulator: Scapy** \u2014 Junos will not originate this condition on its own.\n\n## Test Steps\n\n1. Configure R1 and R2 in Area 0.0.0.0 on the shared interface with a known, matching hello-interval (e.g. 10s) and dead-interval, and let the adjacency form.\n2. On R1 (DUT), verify via PyEZ that the neighbor R2 reaches and remains in Full state, which requires R1 to be sending Hellos at HelloInterval.\n3. In parallel, run a Scapy sniffer on the segment to capture OSPF Hello packets sourced from R1 over ~3-4 intervals and assert the measured mean inter-Hello gap equals the configured HelloInterval within tolerance (external observation, not expressible via the allowed PyEZ points).\n\n## Expected Result\n\n1. The OSPF adjacency to R2 reaches Full state, confirming R1 is emitting Hellos regularly enough for the peer to complete and hold the adjacency (indirect proof Hellos are being sent at HelloInterval).\n2. A neighbor is actually present/identified (an adjacency exists to observe), so the Full-state check is meaningful rather than vacuously passing on an empty neighbor table.\n3. The exact inter-Hello transmission interval from R1 equals the configured HelloInterval (within tolerance), measured from Scapy wire captures of successive Hello packets. Not expressible through get_ospf_neighbor_information/get_ospf_database_information, so no PyEZ assertion is provided.\n\n**Observation point:** `rpc.get_ospf_neighbor_information() -> .//neighbor-state`\n\n**Additional observations captured:**\n- `neighbor_id` = `.//ospf-neighbor/neighbor-id` via the primary observation above\n\n## Safety\n\n- Lab-only execution: **Yes**\n- Requires `commit confirmed` on configuration changes: **Yes**\n\n## Traceability\n\n- Knowledge base: `kb/knowledge.db` (retrieved, not re-derived from raw RFC text)\n\n\n## Notes\n\nThe literal 'at HelloInterval seconds' timing requirement is only verifiable via packet capture (Scapy sniffer); the allowed PyEZ observation points (neighbor info, LSDB) cannot measure per-packet timing, so that check is documented but non-executable here. The PyEZ checks only prove Hellos are flowing (adjacency Full), not their precise cadence. Confidence is medium because the core normative timing fact depends on the external sniffer, not the automatically-fetched PyEZ data. get_ospf_interface_information (which would expose the configured hello-interval) is not in the allowed observation menu, so the configured value cannot be cross-checked here.\n\n","emulator_tool":"Scapy","generation_mode":"ai-medium","keyword":"MUST","needs_review":1,"priority":"medium","protocol_reasoning":"OSPF's Hello protocol requires each router to transmit a Hello packet out every enabled interface once per HelloInterval; these periodic Hellos are what establish and sustain neighbor relationships and reset the peer's RouterDeadInterval timer. If a Junos DUT (R1) is emitting Hellos at the correct cadence, its peer (R2) will progress and hold the adjacency at Full; a sustained Full state is indirect proof that Hellos are flowing continuously, but the exact inter-packet interval is only measurable on the wire.","pytest_content":"\"\"\"\nAuto-generated pytest/PyEZ stub -- traced to RFC2328-S3-REQ-01\nRFC 2328 (OSPF Version 2), Section 3 (Interface Hello Protocol)\nGeneration mode: ai-medium\n\nRequirement (MUST): A router MUST send Hello packets out each OSPF-enabled interface at HelloInterval seconds.\n\nProtocol reasoning: OSPF's Hello protocol requires each router to transmit a Hello packet out every enabled interface once per HelloInterval; these periodic Hellos are what establish and sustain neighbor relationships and reset the peer's RouterDeadInterval timer. If a Junos DUT (R1) is emitting Hellos at the correct cadence, its peer (R2) will progress and hold the adjacency at Full; a sustained Full state is indirect proof that Hellos are flowing continuously, but the exact inter-packet interval is only measurable on the wire.\n\nSTATUS: lab-ready stub. Uses real PyEZ call patterns but device host/credentials\nare placeholders -- wire to your vJunos-router/vMX lab inventory before running.\n\nREQUIRES PEER EMULATOR: Scapy -- Junos will not originate this condition on its own; this stub alone cannot exercise this requirement.\n\"\"\"\nimport pytest\nfrom jnpr.junos import Device\nfrom jnpr.junos.utils.config import Config\n\n\nREQUIREMENT_ID = \"RFC2328-S3-REQ-01\"\nTEST_TYPE = \"positive\"\nRISK = \"medium\"\nGENERATION_MODE = \"ai-medium\"\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef r1():\n    dev = Device(host=\"R1_LAB_HOST\", user=\"LAB_USER\", password=\"LAB_PASSWORD\", port=22)\n    dev.open()\n    yield dev\n    dev.close()\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef r2():\n    dev = Device(host=\"R2_LAB_HOST\", user=\"LAB_USER\", password=\"LAB_PASSWORD\", port=22)\n    dev.open()\n    yield dev\n    dev.close()\n\n\ndef test_rfc2328_s3_req_01(r1, r2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Traces to: RFC2328-S3-REQ-01\n    A router MUST send Hello packets out each OSPF-enabled interface at HelloInterval seconds.\n\n    Reasoning: OSPF's Hello protocol requires each router to transmit a Hello packet out every enabled interface once per HelloInterval; these periodic Hellos are what establish and sustain neighbor relationships and reset the peer's RouterDeadInterval timer. If a Junos DUT (R1) is emitting Hellos at the correct cadence, its peer (R2) will progress and hold the adjacency at Full; a sustained Full state is indirect proof that Hellos are flowing continuously, but the exact inter-packet interval is only measurable on the wire.\n    \"\"\"\n    with Config(r1, mode=\"exclusive\") as cu:\n        cu.load(\n            '''\nprotocols {\n    ospf {\n        area 0.0.0.0 {\n            interface ge-0/0/0.0 {\n                hello-interval 10;\n                dead-interval 40;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n            ''',\n            format=\"text\",\n        )\n        cu.commit(confirm=2)\n\n    # --- stimulus ---\n    # NOTE: requires a peer emulator (Scapy) to construct this stimulus --\n    # PyEZ/Junos alone cannot originate a non-conformant message.\n    # TODO: send the crafted stimulus via Scapy here.\n\n    # --- observation ---\n    # PyEZ observation point (AI-suggested): rpc.get_ospf_neighbor_information() -> .//neighbor-state\n    _info = r1.rpc.get_ospf_neighbor_information()\n    neighbor_state = _info.findtext(\".//neighbor-state\")\n\n    # additional AI-requested observation(s)\n    neighbor_id = _info.findtext('.//ospf-neighbor/neighbor-id')\n\n    # --- assertion ---\n    # Expected checks:\n    #   1. The OSPF adjacency to R2 reaches Full state, confirming R1 is emitting Hellos regularly enough for the peer to complete and hold the adjacency (indirect proof Hellos are being sent at HelloInterval).\n    #   2. A neighbor is actually present/identified (an adjacency exists to observe), so the Full-state check is meaningful rather than vacuously passing on an empty neighbor table.\n    #   3. The exact inter-Hello transmission interval from R1 equals the configured HelloInterval (within tolerance), measured from Scapy wire captures of successive Hello packets. Not expressible through get_ospf_neighbor_information/get_ospf_database_information, so no PyEZ assertion is provided.\n    assert neighbor_state is not None, \"Could not read neighbor_state via PyEZ RPC\"\n    assert neighbor_state == 'Full', \"The OSPF adjacency to R2 reaches Full state, confirming R1 is emitting Hellos regularly enough for the peer to complete and hold the adjacency (indirect proof Hellos are being sent at HelloInterval). (medium confidence) -- verify against lab output\"\n    assert neighbor_id is not None and neighbor_id != '', \"A neighbor is actually present/identified (an adjacency exists to observe), so the Full-state check is meaningful rather than vacuously passing on an empty neighbor table. (medium confidence) -- verify against lab output\"\n    # TODO: The exact inter-Hello transmission interval from R1 equals the configured HelloInterval (within tolerance), measured from Scapy wire captures of successive Hello packets. Not expressible through get_ospf_neighbor_information/get_ospf_database_information, so no PyEZ assertion is provided.\n    # assert neighbor_state == \"<expected value for this check>\"\n","requirement_id":"RFC2328-S3-REQ-01","requires_peer_emulator":1,"risk":"medium","section_id":"3","section_title":"Interface Hello Protocol","statement":"A router MUST send Hello packets out each OSPF-enabled interface at HelloInterval seconds.","test_id":"rfc2328_s3_req_01","test_type":"positive","timers":"{\"hello_interval\": 10, \"dead_interval\": 40, \"retransmit_interval\": 5}","topology":"two-router-single-area"}

